WEBVTT

00:00:05.306 --> 00:00:10.306
So imagine your entire city's grocery budget,

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:12.866
like the funding that dictates

00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:14.746
whether the supermarkets stay open

00:00:14.746 --> 00:00:16.346
or shut down entirely.

00:00:16.346 --> 00:00:18.306
Imagine if that was decided by the receipts

00:00:18.306 --> 00:00:22.426
of just 30 vegans living in one very specific

00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:23.546
isolated neighborhood.

00:00:23.546 --> 00:00:24.906
Well, I mean, that would be catastrophic.

00:00:24.906 --> 00:00:26.146
The shelves would be empty

00:00:26.146 --> 00:00:27.626
for everybody else within a week.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:29.586
Right, you'd be looking at this tiny,

00:00:29.586 --> 00:00:31.346
incredibly specific sample size

00:00:31.346 --> 00:00:34.706
and using it to dictate how much a family

00:00:34.706 --> 00:00:37.226
of five across town can spend on milk and eggs.

00:00:37.226 --> 00:00:39.146
Yeah, it's a mathematical glitch

00:00:39.146 --> 00:00:41.066
that just defies common sense.

00:00:41.066 --> 00:00:42.066
Exactly.

00:00:42.066 --> 00:00:45.226
But today, in this deep dive into the source material,

00:00:45.226 --> 00:00:47.826
we are looking at how a statistical absurdity

00:00:47.826 --> 00:00:50.146
exactly like that is currently threatening

00:00:50.146 --> 00:00:55.026
the healthcare of about 676,000 American citizens

00:00:55.026 --> 00:00:55.866
in Puerto Rico.

00:00:55.866 --> 00:00:56.706
And it really all comes down to

00:00:56.706 --> 00:00:58.706
a hidden automated formula running

00:00:58.706 --> 00:01:00.666
deep inside the federal government.

00:01:00.666 --> 00:01:03.106
A formula that essentially dictates life or death

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:05.786
for local hospitals, clinics, and pharmacies.

00:01:05.786 --> 00:01:07.666
I wanna get straight into the mechanics of this

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:10.746
because when I was looking through our stack of material,

00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:14.066
we have these official 26 letters

00:01:14.066 --> 00:01:16.106
to the Department of Health and Human Services,

00:01:16.106 --> 00:01:18.946
or HHS specifically,

00:01:18.946 --> 00:01:22.066
to Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr.

00:01:22.066 --> 00:01:25.186
and to the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services,

00:01:25.186 --> 00:01:28.626
CMS, run by administrator Dr. Mehmet Oz.

00:01:28.626 --> 00:01:30.826
We've got internal strategy memos.

00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:32.986
We've got a massive push from local industry.

00:01:32.986 --> 00:01:35.746
And the core of the entire crisis,

00:01:35.746 --> 00:01:39.466
it boils down to what is essentially a broken thermometer.

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:40.906
That is a perfect way to look at it

00:01:40.906 --> 00:01:43.666
because to understand why the thermometer is broken,

00:01:44.906 --> 00:01:47.266
we have to look at the patient it's trying to measure.

00:01:47.266 --> 00:01:49.106
In Puerto Rico, Medicare Advantage

00:01:49.106 --> 00:01:51.546
is not just a popular insurance option.

00:01:51.546 --> 00:01:53.666
It is the absolute backbone

00:01:53.666 --> 00:01:56.106
of the entire medical system there.

00:01:56.106 --> 00:01:57.786
Yeah, the numbers here are just wild.

00:01:57.786 --> 00:02:01.626
There are approximately 676,000 beneficiaries

00:02:01.626 --> 00:02:04.066
enrolled in Medicare Advantage plans on the island.

00:02:04.066 --> 00:02:05.946
Which is roughly 96%

00:02:05.946 --> 00:02:08.666
of the entire Medicare eligible population there.

00:02:08.666 --> 00:02:11.626
Just to contrast that with the mainland United States,

00:02:11.626 --> 00:02:14.186
here, Medicare Advantage penetration

00:02:14.186 --> 00:02:16.386
hovers around 50% give or take.

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:17.546
Okay, so it's a choice here.

00:02:17.546 --> 00:02:18.506
Exactly.

00:02:18.506 --> 00:02:20.146
You have a choice between traditional,

00:02:20.146 --> 00:02:23.626
original Medicare or private Medicare Advantage plan.

00:02:23.626 --> 00:02:26.386
But in Puerto Rico, virtually everyone is an advantage.

00:02:26.386 --> 00:02:28.186
And a massive chunk of that population

00:02:28.186 --> 00:02:29.706
is incredibly vulnerable, right?

00:02:29.706 --> 00:02:34.266
Like over 305,000 of them are low income beneficiaries

00:02:34.266 --> 00:02:37.426
who are duly eligible for both Medicare and Medicaid.

00:02:37.426 --> 00:02:41.106
Yes, so you have this highly vulnerable,

00:02:41.106 --> 00:02:43.026
aging low income population

00:02:43.026 --> 00:02:45.106
relying on the single mechanism.

00:02:45.106 --> 00:02:47.186
And this is where the federal math problem,

00:02:47.186 --> 00:02:48.826
the broken thermometer comes into play.

00:02:48.826 --> 00:02:49.746
Okay.

00:02:49.746 --> 00:02:52.066
By law, the federal government has to figure out

00:02:52.066 --> 00:02:54.786
how much money to give a Medicare Advantage plan

00:02:54.786 --> 00:02:57.106
to take care of a patient in a specific county.

00:02:57.106 --> 00:02:58.546
They call this a benchmark.

00:02:58.546 --> 00:03:00.666
Okay, so like a base rate, how do they calculate it?

00:03:00.666 --> 00:03:02.506
Well, they base it on the local costs

00:03:02.506 --> 00:03:04.426
of traditional fee for service Medicare,

00:03:04.426 --> 00:03:05.546
original Medicare.

00:03:05.546 --> 00:03:08.786
The logic historically is pretty straightforward.

00:03:08.786 --> 00:03:11.906
CMS looks at how much it costs to treat a patient

00:03:11.906 --> 00:03:15.026
in the traditional government run system in a given area.

00:03:15.026 --> 00:03:18.346
And they use that data to set the payment benchmark

00:03:18.346 --> 00:03:20.466
for the private Medicare Advantage plans

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:21.746
in that same area.

00:03:21.746 --> 00:03:22.986
Wait, hold on a second.

00:03:22.986 --> 00:03:26.626
You just said 96% of the island uses Medicare Advantage.

00:03:26.626 --> 00:03:27.546
I did.

00:03:27.546 --> 00:03:29.146
So how can you base a benchmark

00:03:29.146 --> 00:03:31.546
on traditional fee for service costs

00:03:31.546 --> 00:03:33.386
when there is virtually nobody

00:03:33.386 --> 00:03:34.906
using traditional fee for service?

00:03:34.906 --> 00:03:37.386
You've hit the exact nerve of the crisis.

00:03:37.386 --> 00:03:38.226
Really?

00:03:38.226 --> 00:03:41.386
Yeah, because almost everyone is in Medicare Advantage.

00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:43.066
There is hardly anyone left

00:03:43.066 --> 00:03:45.106
in the traditional fee for service pool.

00:03:45.106 --> 00:03:48.426
There are only about 29,000 beneficiaries remaining

00:03:48.426 --> 00:03:51.466
in original Medicare Parts A and B in Puerto Rico.

00:03:51.466 --> 00:03:53.706
That's the 30 vegans from my analogy.

00:03:53.706 --> 00:03:54.546
Exactly.

00:03:54.546 --> 00:03:57.786
You are legally basing a multi-billion dollar payout,

00:03:57.786 --> 00:04:00.466
the survival of over 600,000 people

00:04:00.466 --> 00:04:02.746
on the data of 29,000 people.

00:04:02.746 --> 00:04:04.586
I mean, how is that even statistically sound?

00:04:04.586 --> 00:04:05.426
It isn't.

00:04:05.426 --> 00:04:06.946
Wouldn't an actuary look at that

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:09.746
and immediately flag it as an invalid sample size?

00:04:09.746 --> 00:04:11.826
They absolutely would, especially because

00:04:11.826 --> 00:04:14.306
it's not even a randomized representative sample.

00:04:14.306 --> 00:04:15.146
Right.

00:04:15.146 --> 00:04:16.866
Those remaining 29,000 people

00:04:16.866 --> 00:04:19.106
in Puerto Rico's traditional Medicare pool

00:04:19.106 --> 00:04:21.266
are fundamentally skewed.

00:04:22.226 --> 00:04:24.906
Well, they are disproportionately individuals

00:04:24.906 --> 00:04:27.386
who have alternative coverage arrangements.

00:04:27.386 --> 00:04:30.386
For example, a large number of them are veterans

00:04:30.386 --> 00:04:33.026
who primarily use the VA healthcare system

00:04:33.026 --> 00:04:35.226
for their actual care.

00:04:35.226 --> 00:04:36.066
Oh, I see.

00:04:36.066 --> 00:04:37.746
But technically, they are still enrolled

00:04:37.746 --> 00:04:38.746
in original Medicare.

00:04:38.746 --> 00:04:40.186
So they aren't generating claims.

00:04:40.186 --> 00:04:41.026
Exactly.

00:04:41.026 --> 00:04:43.186
They aren't going to the local civilian hospital

00:04:43.186 --> 00:04:44.746
and running up the kinds of bills

00:04:44.746 --> 00:04:47.226
a typical eight-year-old with a heart condition would.

00:04:47.226 --> 00:04:48.146
No, they aren't.

00:04:48.146 --> 00:04:49.946
Or you have populations

00:04:49.946 --> 00:04:52.946
that simply don't utilize care as frequently

00:04:52.946 --> 00:04:56.186
or there are issues with incomplete claims capture.

00:04:56.186 --> 00:04:59.586
Whatever the specific micro-reason, the macro-result...

00:04:59.586 --> 00:05:00.946
Right, I see where this is going.

00:05:00.946 --> 00:05:04.626
The federal algorithm looks at this tiny anomalous group

00:05:04.626 --> 00:05:05.946
of 29,000 people,

00:05:05.946 --> 00:05:07.786
sees that they aren't spending much money,

00:05:07.786 --> 00:05:09.746
and the computer just spits out the conclusion

00:05:09.746 --> 00:05:12.866
like, wow, healthcare in Puerto Rico must be dirt cheap.

00:05:12.866 --> 00:05:13.906
Basically, yeah.

00:05:13.906 --> 00:05:17.066
It says, let's set the Medicare Advantage benchmark

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:21.426
super low for the other 676,000 people.

00:05:21.426 --> 00:05:22.266
Wow.

00:05:22.266 --> 00:05:23.706
That is exactly what happens.

00:05:23.706 --> 00:05:26.066
It's like reading the temperature of a freezer

00:05:26.066 --> 00:05:28.106
and applying it to the entire house.

00:05:28.106 --> 00:05:29.586
But the house isn't freezing.

00:05:29.586 --> 00:05:32.706
I mean, the real costs of running a hospital,

00:05:32.706 --> 00:05:35.346
paying for MRI machines, buying pharmaceuticals,

00:05:35.346 --> 00:05:37.506
paying doctors, none of that is actually cheaper, right?

00:05:37.506 --> 00:05:38.586
It's often more expensive

00:05:38.586 --> 00:05:40.186
because it's an island economy

00:05:40.186 --> 00:05:43.186
with massive supply chain and utility costs.

00:05:43.186 --> 00:05:45.426
Yet, because of this flawed formula,

00:05:45.426 --> 00:05:48.266
Puerto Rico operates at the lowest Medicare Advantage

00:05:48.266 --> 00:05:50.586
benchmark levels in the entire nation.

00:05:50.586 --> 00:05:51.546
How low?

00:05:51.546 --> 00:05:55.306
It is approximately 41% below the national average.

00:05:55.306 --> 00:05:56.546
41%.

00:05:56.546 --> 00:05:57.826
I just want to pause on that

00:05:57.826 --> 00:06:02.666
because it's hard to conceptualize a gap that massive.

00:06:02.666 --> 00:06:06.186
You cannot mandate that a hospital operate on 60 cents

00:06:06.186 --> 00:06:09.106
on the dollar for a decade and expect it to survive.

00:06:09.106 --> 00:06:09.946
You just can't.

00:06:09.946 --> 00:06:10.786
No, you can't.

00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:11.946
And it gets even more baffling

00:06:11.946 --> 00:06:13.866
when you look geographically next door.

00:06:13.866 --> 00:06:16.586
Puerto Rico's benchmark is 20% below the benchmarks

00:06:16.586 --> 00:06:18.186
in the U.S. Virgin Islands.

00:06:18.186 --> 00:06:19.026
Wait, really?

00:06:19.026 --> 00:06:20.866
But the Virgin Islands are right there.

00:06:20.866 --> 00:06:24.186
They face the exact same island economy constraints,

00:06:24.186 --> 00:06:28.586
shipping logistics, hurricane threats, power grid issues.

00:06:28.586 --> 00:06:30.706
Why is their benchmark so much higher?

00:06:30.706 --> 00:06:32.626
Well, that discrepancy is actually the core

00:06:32.626 --> 00:06:34.666
of the political and administrative fight

00:06:34.666 --> 00:06:35.706
happening right now.

00:06:35.706 --> 00:06:37.866
It leads directly to the massive pushback

00:06:37.866 --> 00:06:39.506
from the local government.

00:06:39.506 --> 00:06:44.066
Specifically, the letter sent on February 25th, 2026

00:06:44.066 --> 00:06:46.706
by Puerto Rico's governor, Jennifer Gonzalez-Colin.

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:49.746
This is the letter to HHS Secretary Kennedy

00:06:49.746 --> 00:06:52.466
and CMS administrator, Dr. Oz.

00:06:52.466 --> 00:06:53.506
I was reading through it

00:06:53.506 --> 00:06:55.266
and she doesn't just lay out the grievance,

00:06:55.266 --> 00:06:57.946
she demands a very specific fix.

00:06:57.946 --> 00:06:59.546
Right, she points directly

00:06:59.546 --> 00:07:02.866
to the agency's existing administrative power.

00:07:02.866 --> 00:07:04.626
She requested the establishment

00:07:04.626 --> 00:07:07.546
of a national Medicare Advantage benchmark floor

00:07:07.546 --> 00:07:10.626
of no less than 0.70.

00:07:10.626 --> 00:07:12.466
Meaning 70% of the national average.

00:07:12.466 --> 00:07:13.426
Exactly.

00:07:13.426 --> 00:07:16.426
She's arguing that no jurisdiction in the United States,

00:07:16.426 --> 00:07:18.666
regardless of what the flawed fee for service

00:07:18.666 --> 00:07:19.986
formula spits out,

00:07:19.986 --> 00:07:23.226
should ever fall below 70% of the national average.

00:07:23.226 --> 00:07:25.466
But why 0.70?

00:07:25.466 --> 00:07:27.986
Is that just like a negotiation tactic?

00:07:27.986 --> 00:07:30.986
Like I'll ask for 70% hoping they give me 60%.

00:07:30.986 --> 00:07:32.666
It's not arbitrary at all.

00:07:32.666 --> 00:07:35.106
0.70 is highly strategic

00:07:35.106 --> 00:07:36.466
because it mirrors the current

00:07:36.466 --> 00:07:37.866
Medicare Advantage payment levels

00:07:37.866 --> 00:07:40.266
already utilized in the U.S. Virgin Islands.

00:07:40.266 --> 00:07:42.946
Oh, so the federal precedent already exists.

00:07:42.946 --> 00:07:45.586
Right, the federal government through CMS

00:07:45.586 --> 00:07:49.026
has already acknowledged that the baseline cost of care

00:07:49.026 --> 00:07:50.346
in the Caribbean territories

00:07:50.346 --> 00:07:52.146
shouldn't dip below a certain threshold.

00:07:52.146 --> 00:07:53.026
I get it.

00:07:53.026 --> 00:07:54.626
The governor is essentially saying

00:07:54.626 --> 00:07:56.866
to Dr. Oz and Secretary Kennedy,

00:07:56.866 --> 00:07:59.826
just apply your own established logic to us.

00:07:59.826 --> 00:08:00.746
And what's brilliant about that,

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:02.906
at least from a strategic standpoint,

00:08:02.946 --> 00:08:07.106
is that she isn't asking Congress to pass a new law.

00:08:07.106 --> 00:08:08.866
Anyone who follows Washington

00:08:08.866 --> 00:08:11.426
knows that getting a massive multi-year

00:08:11.426 --> 00:08:13.666
legislative overhaul through Congress

00:08:13.666 --> 00:08:15.226
is nearly impossible right now.

00:08:15.226 --> 00:08:16.906
Oh, it's a bureaucratic nightmare.

00:08:16.906 --> 00:08:19.066
But an administrative rule change.

00:08:19.066 --> 00:08:22.106
CMS could theoretically tweak the formula

00:08:22.106 --> 00:08:23.986
in their next rulemaking cycle.

00:08:23.986 --> 00:08:25.306
They can just say, you know,

00:08:25.306 --> 00:08:28.386
the data is invalid, we are instituting a floor.

00:08:28.386 --> 00:08:29.626
But they haven't.

00:08:29.626 --> 00:08:30.706
And in the meantime,

00:08:30.706 --> 00:08:33.226
the reality on the ground is getting grim.

00:08:33.226 --> 00:08:35.306
If I'm a clinic administrator in San Juan

00:08:35.306 --> 00:08:38.506
and my revenue is artificially depressed by 41%,

00:08:38.506 --> 00:08:39.946
I'm purely in survival mode.

00:08:39.946 --> 00:08:42.066
Yeah, you are deferring maintenance.

00:08:42.066 --> 00:08:43.826
The sources highlight that providers

00:08:43.826 --> 00:08:46.586
face chronic existential underpayment.

00:08:46.586 --> 00:08:48.626
They cannot reliably invest in infrastructure.

00:08:48.626 --> 00:08:50.426
You can't repair aging equipment.

00:08:50.426 --> 00:08:52.706
You definitely struggle with workforce retention.

00:08:52.706 --> 00:08:53.546
Well, for sure.

00:08:53.546 --> 00:08:55.786
If you are a specialized nurse or a doctor

00:08:55.786 --> 00:08:58.186
and the local hospital can only pay you a fraction

00:08:58.186 --> 00:08:59.706
of what you'd make in Florida or Texas

00:08:59.746 --> 00:09:02.146
because their federal reimbursements are slashed,

00:09:02.146 --> 00:09:02.986
you leave.

00:09:02.986 --> 00:09:04.146
So you get massive brain drain.

00:09:04.146 --> 00:09:05.346
Massive brain drain.

00:09:05.346 --> 00:09:07.946
And that ultimately hits the patient.

00:09:07.946 --> 00:09:10.946
Beneficiaries face increasing pressure on benefits,

00:09:10.946 --> 00:09:13.906
narrower networks, and longer wait times for care.

00:09:13.906 --> 00:09:14.946
It's just snowballs.

00:09:14.946 --> 00:09:16.746
Yeah, there was even a moment recently,

00:09:16.746 --> 00:09:18.666
sort of a canary in the coal mine,

00:09:18.666 --> 00:09:22.306
where one significant Medicare Advantage plan

00:09:22.306 --> 00:09:24.386
pulled out of the Puerto Rico market entirely.

00:09:24.386 --> 00:09:27.066
Which is terrifying when 96% of your seniors

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:28.426
rely on that specific market.

00:09:28.426 --> 00:09:29.826
That's a vicious cycle.

00:09:29.826 --> 00:09:31.506
But the governor wasn't fighting

00:09:31.506 --> 00:09:33.466
this Washington math alone, right?

00:09:33.466 --> 00:09:36.026
She needed the people actually delivering the care

00:09:36.026 --> 00:09:36.986
to back her up.

00:09:36.986 --> 00:09:37.826
Right.

00:09:37.826 --> 00:09:39.706
I mean, a politician's letter is one thing,

00:09:39.706 --> 00:09:41.986
but an entire industry unified

00:09:41.986 --> 00:09:44.746
behind a specific technical fix is another.

00:09:44.746 --> 00:09:47.106
This is where the health care coalition steps up.

00:09:47.106 --> 00:09:48.986
And we should clarify, the health care coalition

00:09:48.986 --> 00:09:51.226
is this massive shared commitment

00:09:51.226 --> 00:09:53.586
among public and private stakeholders on the island.

00:09:53.586 --> 00:09:55.626
Hospitals, doctors, pharmacies, you name it.

00:09:55.626 --> 00:09:58.146
And on May 18th, 2026,

00:09:58.186 --> 00:10:00.466
the health care coalition signed a formal memorandum

00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:02.506
of understanding and MOU,

00:10:02.506 --> 00:10:04.506
outlining their absolute top tier

00:10:04.506 --> 00:10:06.986
federal health care policy priorities.

00:10:06.986 --> 00:10:08.826
And right at the top of that,

00:10:08.826 --> 00:10:11.786
MOU was official industry wide backing

00:10:11.786 --> 00:10:16.786
for the 0.70 benchmark floor for the 2028 rate cycle.

00:10:17.426 --> 00:10:19.826
The entire local health care sector

00:10:19.826 --> 00:10:22.386
threw its weight behind the governor's proposal.

00:10:22.386 --> 00:10:23.866
But the MOU goes further,

00:10:23.866 --> 00:10:25.386
and this is where I actually got a little lost

00:10:25.386 --> 00:10:26.386
in the weeds of the policy.

00:10:26.386 --> 00:10:28.066
So I want you to walk me through this part.

00:10:28.906 --> 00:10:31.906
They don't just talk about the Medicare Advantage benchmark.

00:10:31.906 --> 00:10:34.226
They highlight a terrifying deadline,

00:10:34.226 --> 00:10:38.626
September 30th, 2027, the Medicaid funding cliff.

00:10:38.626 --> 00:10:41.866
How does Medicaid tie into the Medicare Advantage problem?

00:10:41.866 --> 00:10:43.506
They are deeply intertwined

00:10:43.506 --> 00:10:45.826
because you have over 305,000 people

00:10:45.826 --> 00:10:48.626
who are duly eligible for both programs.

00:10:48.626 --> 00:10:49.626
The health care coalition

00:10:49.626 --> 00:10:51.386
isn't just looking at a broken benchmark,

00:10:51.386 --> 00:10:53.306
they are looking at a perfect storm.

00:10:53.306 --> 00:10:55.426
Puerto Rico's Medicaid system operates

00:10:55.426 --> 00:10:57.426
under intense structural constraints

00:10:57.426 --> 00:10:59.826
that mainland states simply do not face.

00:10:59.826 --> 00:11:01.586
Okay, let's break that down.

00:11:01.586 --> 00:11:04.266
How is a Medicaid program in a territory

00:11:04.266 --> 00:11:06.666
different from a state like Ohio or Oregon?

00:11:06.666 --> 00:11:08.866
Well, there are two massive differences.

00:11:08.866 --> 00:11:11.346
First, Puerto Rico has a capped allotment.

00:11:11.346 --> 00:11:12.946
Meaning a hard ceiling on the money.

00:11:12.946 --> 00:11:13.906
Yes.

00:11:13.906 --> 00:11:14.986
The federal government says

00:11:14.986 --> 00:11:16.866
here is a specific block of money

00:11:16.866 --> 00:11:18.946
for your Medicaid program for the year.

00:11:18.946 --> 00:11:20.506
When it's gone, it's gone.

00:11:20.506 --> 00:11:21.346
Wow.

00:11:21.346 --> 00:11:22.466
In a state on the mainland,

00:11:22.466 --> 00:11:25.186
Medicaid is an open-ended entitlement.

00:11:25.186 --> 00:11:27.786
If a recession hits or a pandemic happens

00:11:27.786 --> 00:11:31.346
and suddenly thousands more people qualify for Medicaid,

00:11:31.346 --> 00:11:33.506
the federal funding scales up automatically

00:11:33.506 --> 00:11:34.746
to match the need.

00:11:34.746 --> 00:11:35.946
But in Puerto Rico,

00:11:35.946 --> 00:11:38.626
if there's a hurricane and a massive spike in need,

00:11:38.626 --> 00:11:41.306
the federal government just shrugs and points to the cap.

00:11:41.306 --> 00:11:42.466
Exactly.

00:11:42.466 --> 00:11:45.026
The local government has to somehow figure out

00:11:45.026 --> 00:11:47.026
how to stretch that capped amount.

00:11:47.026 --> 00:11:50.106
And the second major difference is the matching rate,

00:11:50.106 --> 00:11:53.586
the federal medical assistance percentage, or FMAP.

00:11:53.586 --> 00:11:54.746
The FMAP.

00:11:54.746 --> 00:11:56.906
Basically, how much the federal government chips in

00:11:56.906 --> 00:11:59.466
versus how much the local government has to pay?

00:11:59.466 --> 00:12:00.706
Right. For Puerto Rico,

00:12:00.706 --> 00:12:02.946
that match is restricted to 76%.

00:12:02.946 --> 00:12:05.186
Meanwhile, all other U.S. territories,

00:12:05.186 --> 00:12:08.146
Guam, American Samoa, the Virgin Islands,

00:12:08.146 --> 00:12:11.626
they received a permanent FMAPIX at 83%

00:12:11.626 --> 00:12:14.666
back in the Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2023.

00:12:14.666 --> 00:12:15.506
Wait, really?

00:12:15.506 --> 00:12:16.346
Yeah.

00:12:16.346 --> 00:12:17.426
So Puerto Rico is capped

00:12:17.426 --> 00:12:19.826
and D, they are getting a lower federal match rate

00:12:19.826 --> 00:12:21.426
than their territorial peers.

00:12:21.426 --> 00:12:23.906
That feels incredibly punitive.

00:12:23.906 --> 00:12:26.266
But even beyond the cap and the match rate,

00:12:26.266 --> 00:12:29.546
the MOU from the Health Care Coalition explicitly points out

00:12:29.546 --> 00:12:32.426
that Puerto Rico just outright lacks access

00:12:32.426 --> 00:12:34.706
to certain safety nets.

00:12:34.706 --> 00:12:36.586
They mentioned the Medicare Savings Program,

00:12:36.586 --> 00:12:40.666
the MSP, and long-term services and supports, LTSS.

00:12:40.666 --> 00:12:43.666
Which is staggering when you think about the demographics.

00:12:43.666 --> 00:12:46.346
Almost half of the Medicare Advantage beneficiaries

00:12:46.346 --> 00:12:48.626
on the island are these duly eligible,

00:12:48.626 --> 00:12:51.266
incredibly low-income seniors.

00:12:51.266 --> 00:12:53.626
On the mainland, the Medicare Savings Program

00:12:53.626 --> 00:12:55.266
is what helps cover their premiums,

00:12:55.266 --> 00:12:57.186
their deductibles, and their co-pays.

00:12:57.186 --> 00:12:58.026
Right.

00:12:58.026 --> 00:13:00.506
LTSS is what helps with nursing homes, assisted living,

00:13:00.506 --> 00:13:03.106
and home-based care for the elderly and disabled.

00:13:03.106 --> 00:13:05.626
And Puerto Rico doesn't have the funding for those.

00:13:05.626 --> 00:13:07.106
They don't have the funding to operate them

00:13:07.106 --> 00:13:09.426
at anything resembling the mainland standard

00:13:09.426 --> 00:13:12.066
because of the capped allotment and the lower FMAP.

00:13:12.066 --> 00:13:13.986
Well, no, I think this is the crucial context

00:13:13.986 --> 00:13:17.626
that makes the Health Care Coalition's MOU so powerful.

00:13:17.626 --> 00:13:19.946
They aren't asking for special treatment.

00:13:19.946 --> 00:13:20.946
It's exactly.

00:13:20.946 --> 00:13:22.426
When you really read what they're saying,

00:13:22.426 --> 00:13:24.306
they aren't asking for a handout.

00:13:24.306 --> 00:13:26.666
They are saying, we don't have the safety nets

00:13:26.666 --> 00:13:27.506
everyone else has.

00:13:27.506 --> 00:13:29.226
We don't have the MSP.

00:13:29.226 --> 00:13:30.546
We don't have LTSS.

00:13:30.546 --> 00:13:32.426
We have a capped Medicaid allotment

00:13:32.426 --> 00:13:34.386
that falls off a cliff in 2027.

00:13:34.386 --> 00:13:35.426
Yep.

00:13:35.426 --> 00:13:38.346
You cannot simultaneously starve

00:13:38.346 --> 00:13:42.626
our Medicare Advantage Program with a broken 3% formula.

00:13:42.626 --> 00:13:45.826
It is the only thing keeping the roof from caving in.

00:13:45.826 --> 00:13:48.026
That is the exact dynamic.

00:13:48.026 --> 00:13:51.186
Because Medicaid is chronically underfunded and capped,

00:13:51.186 --> 00:13:54.106
the Medicare Advantage Program is forced to compensate.

00:13:54.106 --> 00:13:55.346
It has to carry the weight.

00:13:55.346 --> 00:13:56.026
Right.

00:13:56.026 --> 00:13:58.626
The private MA plans and the local providers

00:13:58.626 --> 00:14:00.546
have to stretch their resources further

00:14:00.546 --> 00:14:02.666
covering these dual eligible patients

00:14:02.666 --> 00:14:04.546
without the traditional federal supports.

00:14:04.546 --> 00:14:05.306
Right.

00:14:05.306 --> 00:14:08.346
So when the MA benchmark itself is artificially

00:14:08.346 --> 00:14:12.306
slashed by 41% due to an invalid fee for service

00:14:12.306 --> 00:14:15.346
sample, I mean, the strain on the entire island's

00:14:15.346 --> 00:14:17.986
health care infrastructure becomes existential.

00:14:17.986 --> 00:14:20.066
OK, so the local government and the private sector

00:14:20.066 --> 00:14:22.306
health care coalition are perfectly aligned.

00:14:22.306 --> 00:14:23.226
They have the MAF.

00:14:23.226 --> 00:14:27.346
They have the 0.70 solution borrowed from the Virgin Islands.

00:14:27.346 --> 00:14:30.666
They have the terrifying context of the Medicaid cliff.

00:14:30.666 --> 00:14:34.306
But having a flawless logical argument in San Juan

00:14:34.306 --> 00:14:36.986
doesn't automatically change a federal algorithm in Washington,

00:14:36.986 --> 00:14:37.506
DC.

00:14:37.506 --> 00:14:38.306
Far from it.

00:14:38.306 --> 00:14:40.346
An airtight argument is just a piece of paper

00:14:40.346 --> 00:14:41.786
until it has political leverage.

00:14:41.786 --> 00:14:44.506
Which brings us to the internal strategy memo detailing

00:14:44.506 --> 00:14:45.746
the congressional campaign.

00:14:45.746 --> 00:14:46.786
Yes.

00:14:46.786 --> 00:14:48.946
And I love documents like this because it's

00:14:48.946 --> 00:14:51.466
the peak behind the curtain.

00:14:51.466 --> 00:14:55.746
We rarely get to see how a deeply technical, almost academic

00:14:55.746 --> 00:14:58.826
critique of a statistical sampling method

00:14:58.826 --> 00:15:01.946
gets transformed into actionable congressional pressure.

00:15:01.946 --> 00:15:04.306
It requires a massive translation effort.

00:15:04.306 --> 00:15:06.106
You have to take the complex data generated

00:15:06.106 --> 00:15:07.826
by the health care coalition and mold it

00:15:07.826 --> 00:15:09.666
into a politically viable request.

00:15:09.666 --> 00:15:12.466
And the memo lays out a two pronged attack.

00:15:12.466 --> 00:15:15.666
It starts on February 25, 2026.

00:15:15.666 --> 00:15:17.826
This is when Democratic Representative Pablo Jose

00:15:17.826 --> 00:15:18.746
Hernandez stepped up.

00:15:19.186 --> 00:15:21.106
He managed to get 17 other Democrats

00:15:21.106 --> 00:15:23.706
to sign on to a letter to CMS, urging them

00:15:23.706 --> 00:15:27.186
to address these specific disparities in the CY 2027

00:15:27.186 --> 00:15:28.146
advance notice.

00:15:28.146 --> 00:15:30.906
It was a very strong opening salvo.

00:15:30.906 --> 00:15:33.986
The letter laid out the exact arguments we just discussed,

00:15:33.986 --> 00:15:38.186
the flawed 3% formula, the lack of safety net programs

00:15:38.186 --> 00:15:41.906
like the MSP and LTSS, the impending Medicaid cliff.

00:15:41.906 --> 00:15:45.066
Having 18 members of Congress tell a federal agency

00:15:45.066 --> 00:15:48.866
that its math is fundamentally broken is significant.

00:15:48.866 --> 00:15:49.986
But it's not enough.

00:15:49.986 --> 00:15:50.826
No.

00:15:50.826 --> 00:15:53.666
The memo makes it clear that the strategic brilliance

00:15:53.666 --> 00:15:58.306
was recognizing that a purely partisan push wouldn't cut it.

00:15:58.306 --> 00:15:59.066
Exactly.

00:15:59.066 --> 00:16:01.266
They needed a Republican-led initiative

00:16:01.266 --> 00:16:03.106
to pair with the Democratic effort

00:16:03.106 --> 00:16:05.546
to show that this was a fundamental issue of fairness

00:16:05.546 --> 00:16:08.826
and efficiency that appealed across the entire aisle.

00:16:08.826 --> 00:16:10.106
And just to be clear, for everyone

00:16:10.106 --> 00:16:12.346
listening to this deep dive, we are maintaining

00:16:12.346 --> 00:16:13.466
strict neutrality here.

00:16:13.466 --> 00:16:14.146
Absolutely.

00:16:14.146 --> 00:16:16.506
We're just exploring the arguments presented

00:16:16.506 --> 00:16:18.466
in these source documents from both Democrats

00:16:18.466 --> 00:16:19.306
and Republicans.

00:16:19.306 --> 00:16:21.266
We're not endorsing any political side.

00:16:21.266 --> 00:16:22.266
Right, exactly.

00:16:22.266 --> 00:16:23.666
We're looking at the strategy.

00:16:23.666 --> 00:16:26.626
And orchestrating that kind of bipartisan maneuvering

00:16:26.626 --> 00:16:28.706
requires institutional leadership.

00:16:28.706 --> 00:16:31.146
You can't just have a bunch of hospital executives

00:16:31.146 --> 00:16:32.866
cold-calling members of Congress.

00:16:32.866 --> 00:16:35.226
Enter the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs Administration.

00:16:35.226 --> 00:16:36.346
Exactly.

00:16:36.346 --> 00:16:38.586
This strategy memo gives immense credit

00:16:38.586 --> 00:16:41.066
to the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs Administration

00:16:41.066 --> 00:16:43.066
for this phase of the campaign.

00:16:43.066 --> 00:16:43.826
OK.

00:16:43.826 --> 00:16:46.346
Specifically, two officials are highlighted

00:16:46.346 --> 00:16:49.866
for giving the effort its institutional direction,

00:16:49.866 --> 00:16:53.906
Gabriela Befeli, the executive director, and Natalia Gandia.

00:16:53.906 --> 00:16:55.386
What exactly did they do?

00:16:55.386 --> 00:16:59.426
I mean, I know what lobbyists do, but how does an administration

00:16:59.426 --> 00:17:01.986
office turn this into a congressional reality?

00:17:01.986 --> 00:17:05.546
They acted as the translators and the strategists.

00:17:05.546 --> 00:17:08.186
You have the private sector hospitals, pharmacies,

00:17:08.186 --> 00:17:10.026
and doctors in the Health Care Coalition

00:17:10.026 --> 00:17:13.186
generating this highly technical actuarial data.

00:17:13.186 --> 00:17:13.666
Right.

00:17:13.706 --> 00:17:16.266
Befeli and Gandia took that raw data

00:17:16.266 --> 00:17:18.786
and framed it in a way that Washington understands.

00:17:18.786 --> 00:17:19.746
Makes sense.

00:17:19.746 --> 00:17:22.226
The memo says, without the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs

00:17:22.226 --> 00:17:24.706
Administration supplying that sustained institutional

00:17:24.706 --> 00:17:26.426
leadership, this might have just

00:17:26.426 --> 00:17:28.186
remained a credible private sector

00:17:28.186 --> 00:17:30.386
recommendation circulating in the ether.

00:17:30.386 --> 00:17:32.666
They gave it a governmental center of gravity.

00:17:32.666 --> 00:17:33.706
Precisely.

00:17:33.706 --> 00:17:36.626
And they made a very specific strategic choice

00:17:36.626 --> 00:17:39.426
on how to frame the ask to these lawmakers,

00:17:39.426 --> 00:17:41.906
particularly to the Republicans they were courting.

00:17:41.946 --> 00:17:44.626
Right, because pitching a massive new funding

00:17:44.626 --> 00:17:48.186
program to fiscal conservatives is usually a non-starter.

00:17:48.186 --> 00:17:48.946
Exactly.

00:17:48.946 --> 00:17:51.466
Think about how hard it is to get a member of Congress

00:17:51.466 --> 00:17:54.826
to sign on to a letter asking for billions of dollars

00:17:54.826 --> 00:17:57.546
in new appropriations or asking to create

00:17:57.546 --> 00:18:00.306
a massive new federal entitlement program.

00:18:00.306 --> 00:18:01.426
Yeah, they'd run for the hills.

00:18:01.426 --> 00:18:01.706
Right.

00:18:01.706 --> 00:18:04.626
Members are inherently wary of the budget impact.

00:18:04.626 --> 00:18:07.266
But the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs Administration

00:18:07.266 --> 00:18:09.746
framed this not as a demand for new money,

00:18:09.746 --> 00:18:11.706
but as formula modernization.

00:18:11.706 --> 00:18:15.826
Like your algorithm is outdated, it's statistically invalid,

00:18:15.826 --> 00:18:17.666
and it's producing a bad output.

00:18:17.666 --> 00:18:18.546
Yes.

00:18:18.546 --> 00:18:21.106
They pitched it as an administrative remedy.

00:18:21.106 --> 00:18:22.546
They said to these lawmakers, we

00:18:22.546 --> 00:18:25.466
are asking you to write a new check or pass a new tax.

00:18:25.466 --> 00:18:27.426
We are asking you to tell the agency

00:18:27.426 --> 00:18:29.786
to use its existing authority to fix

00:18:29.786 --> 00:18:32.346
a mathematical error that relies on a tiny,

00:18:32.346 --> 00:18:35.186
unrepresentative sample of 29,000 people.

00:18:35.186 --> 00:18:36.146
That's smart.

00:18:36.146 --> 00:18:37.986
Because it was an administrative ask,

00:18:37.986 --> 00:18:40.146
Congress members could sign on safely.

00:18:40.186 --> 00:18:41.946
It's a low risk vehicle for them,

00:18:41.946 --> 00:18:44.786
but a potentially life-saving vehicle for Puerto Rico.

00:18:44.786 --> 00:18:47.146
That is incredibly savvy.

00:18:47.146 --> 00:18:50.626
So who did the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs Administration

00:18:50.626 --> 00:18:52.866
and the Health Care Coalition actually

00:18:52.866 --> 00:18:55.786
get to lead this charge on the Republican side?

00:18:55.786 --> 00:18:58.506
Let's look at the August 3, 2026 letter.

00:18:58.506 --> 00:19:01.066
This is a critical turning point in the timeline.

00:19:01.066 --> 00:19:04.226
14 GOP House members sent a formal letter

00:19:04.226 --> 00:19:07.746
to HHS Secretary RFK Jr. explicitly

00:19:07.746 --> 00:19:12.466
requesting the 0.70 benchmark floor for 2028.

00:19:12.466 --> 00:19:14.946
And the lead signer on this letter is vital.

00:19:14.946 --> 00:19:16.906
It's Representative Gregory F. Murphy,

00:19:16.906 --> 00:19:18.866
a Republican from North Carolina.

00:19:18.866 --> 00:19:19.866
Now, why him?

00:19:19.866 --> 00:19:21.426
I mean, North Carolina isn't exactly

00:19:21.426 --> 00:19:22.466
next door to Puerto Rico.

00:19:22.466 --> 00:19:24.586
Because of his clinical and political profile,

00:19:24.586 --> 00:19:26.346
Dr. Greg Murphy is a physician.

00:19:26.346 --> 00:19:28.266
More importantly, he is the co-chair

00:19:28.266 --> 00:19:30.186
of the House Republican Doctors Caucus.

00:19:30.186 --> 00:19:31.866
And as the sources note, he's not just

00:19:31.866 --> 00:19:34.546
some automatic rubber stamp for the insurance industry.

00:19:34.546 --> 00:19:35.826
Far from it.

00:19:35.826 --> 00:19:37.866
As a physician, he has actually supported

00:19:37.866 --> 00:19:41.386
closer scrutiny of Medicare Advantage practices

00:19:41.386 --> 00:19:44.026
that burden doctors or delay patient care,

00:19:44.026 --> 00:19:45.706
like prior authorization issues.

00:19:45.706 --> 00:19:46.866
OK, so he gets it.

00:19:46.866 --> 00:19:49.706
He looks at things through the lens of a provider.

00:19:49.706 --> 00:19:52.026
So when he steps up and says, we

00:19:52.026 --> 00:19:54.066
need to increase MA funding in Puerto Rico,

00:19:54.066 --> 00:19:57.666
it carries massive, undeniable credibility.

00:19:57.666 --> 00:20:00.706
He's not doing it to pad insurance profits.

00:20:00.706 --> 00:20:03.346
He's doing it because as a doctor,

00:20:03.386 --> 00:20:06.146
he looks at the math and says, this health care system

00:20:06.146 --> 00:20:07.386
is being starved.

00:20:07.386 --> 00:20:11.106
And his connection to the island is actually very direct,

00:20:11.106 --> 00:20:14.106
which I found fascinating in the internal memos.

00:20:14.106 --> 00:20:16.466
Dr. Greg Murphy has been invited by the Chamber

00:20:16.466 --> 00:20:19.586
of Commerce of Puerto Rico as a keynote speaker

00:20:19.586 --> 00:20:22.586
on the health care forum and is close to some leader

00:20:22.586 --> 00:20:25.826
in the health care coalition, including doctors, pharmacy

00:20:25.826 --> 00:20:28.826
owners, and hospital executive, among other.

00:20:28.826 --> 00:20:31.306
Yeah, that on the ground connection is invaluable.

00:20:31.306 --> 00:20:33.266
It's one thing to read a briefing paper provided

00:20:33.266 --> 00:20:35.386
by the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs Administration.

00:20:35.386 --> 00:20:37.066
It's another to have engaged directly

00:20:37.066 --> 00:20:39.386
with the people running the hospitals and the pharmacies

00:20:39.386 --> 00:20:40.386
on the island.

00:20:40.386 --> 00:20:43.026
He understands that the logistical and economic

00:20:43.026 --> 00:20:46.466
conditions in San Juan or Ponce differ fundamentally

00:20:46.466 --> 00:20:48.586
from his district in North Carolina.

00:20:48.586 --> 00:20:51.186
And he wasn't the only heavy hitter they brought in.

00:20:51.186 --> 00:20:53.186
The coalition of 14 Republicans

00:20:53.186 --> 00:20:55.346
was very carefully constructed.

00:20:55.346 --> 00:20:57.746
You had representatives from other territories

00:20:57.746 --> 00:21:00.706
like Amada Coleman-Raidwagon from American Samoa,

00:21:00.706 --> 00:21:04.026
who understands all too well how federal formulas often

00:21:04.026 --> 00:21:06.506
ignore the realities of island territories.

00:21:06.506 --> 00:21:09.586
You also had a strong showing from the Florida delegation.

00:21:09.586 --> 00:21:14.226
Carlos Jimenez, Maria Elvira Salazar, Mario Diaz-Balart.

00:21:14.226 --> 00:21:17.826
They have deep constituency connections to Puerto Rico.

00:21:17.826 --> 00:21:19.746
They know that when the island's health care system

00:21:19.746 --> 00:21:22.826
falters, it directly impacts their districts in Miami

00:21:22.826 --> 00:21:23.706
and beyond.

00:21:23.706 --> 00:21:25.506
And then there's another crucial medical voice

00:21:25.506 --> 00:21:26.626
on the letter.

00:21:26.626 --> 00:21:29.226
Representative Buddy Carter from Georgia,

00:21:29.226 --> 00:21:31.946
Buddy Carter is a pharmacist very close to Trump

00:21:31.946 --> 00:21:33.386
and was recently in Puerto Rico.

00:21:33.386 --> 00:21:35.186
Having a pharmacist on board, especially

00:21:35.186 --> 00:21:38.186
one with strong political ties and recent firsthand experience

00:21:38.186 --> 00:21:40.466
on the island, adds another vital layer

00:21:40.466 --> 00:21:42.826
of clinical and political credibility.

00:21:42.826 --> 00:21:43.826
Makes total sense.

00:21:43.826 --> 00:21:46.426
He understands the supply chain.

00:21:46.426 --> 00:21:49.226
He understands the cost of specialized drugs,

00:21:49.226 --> 00:21:51.426
the logistics of getting them to an island,

00:21:51.426 --> 00:21:54.426
and how chronic underpayment from Medicare Advantage

00:21:54.426 --> 00:21:56.226
affects a local pharmacy's ability

00:21:56.226 --> 00:21:58.186
to simply keep the lights on and stock

00:21:58.186 --> 00:21:59.546
life-saving medications.

00:21:59.546 --> 00:22:01.866
OK, I have to stop and play devil's advocate here.

00:22:01.866 --> 00:22:04.426
Because we have laid out a scenario where the math is

00:22:04.426 --> 00:22:06.226
objectively broken.

00:22:06.226 --> 00:22:08.426
The thermometer is reading the wrong temperature.

00:22:08.426 --> 00:22:11.506
We have the governor pleading for an administrative fix.

00:22:11.506 --> 00:22:13.786
We have the local health care coalition

00:22:13.786 --> 00:22:17.226
showing how this ties into a massive Medicaid cliff.

00:22:17.226 --> 00:22:19.666
We have progressive Democrats writing letters.

00:22:19.666 --> 00:22:22.226
We have conservative Republican doctors and pharmacists

00:22:22.226 --> 00:22:24.546
writing letters marshaled by the Puerto Rico Federal

00:22:24.546 --> 00:22:25.466
Affairs Administration.

00:22:25.506 --> 00:22:26.746
It is a unified front.

00:22:26.746 --> 00:22:27.266
Right.

00:22:27.266 --> 00:22:31.746
So if this formula is so obviously mathematically broken

00:22:31.746 --> 00:22:33.706
and you have bipartisan support spanning

00:22:33.706 --> 00:22:35.986
the entire political spectrum, why

00:22:35.986 --> 00:22:40.106
is it so hard for CMS or HHS to just flip a switch and fix it?

00:22:40.106 --> 00:22:40.626
That is.

00:22:40.626 --> 00:22:43.506
Why does it take this massive multi-year orchestration

00:22:43.506 --> 00:22:46.906
just to change a decimal point in a formula to 0.70?

00:22:46.906 --> 00:22:49.626
That is the multi-billion dollar question.

00:22:49.626 --> 00:22:52.426
And the answer is the terrifying power

00:22:52.426 --> 00:22:53.986
of bureaucratic inertia.

00:22:53.986 --> 00:22:58.066
Bureaucratic inertia, like a body at rest, stays at rest,

00:22:58.066 --> 00:22:59.626
especially if it's a federal agency.

00:22:59.626 --> 00:23:00.706
Exactly.

00:23:00.706 --> 00:23:03.346
Federal algorithms are deeply entrenched.

00:23:03.346 --> 00:23:05.066
They aren't just lines of code.

00:23:05.066 --> 00:23:08.386
They are codified in rule makings, federal registers,

00:23:08.386 --> 00:23:10.866
and policy manuals that have been built up over decades.

00:23:10.866 --> 00:23:11.466
Right.

00:23:11.466 --> 00:23:14.866
To change the formula, you are asking a massive bureaucracy

00:23:14.866 --> 00:23:17.586
to formally admit that its standard operating procedure,

00:23:17.586 --> 00:23:20.066
the way it has done business for years,

00:23:20.066 --> 00:23:22.866
is producing an invalid, harmful result

00:23:22.866 --> 00:23:24.906
for a specific population.

00:23:24.906 --> 00:23:25.506
Ouch.

00:23:25.506 --> 00:23:25.786
Yeah.

00:23:25.786 --> 00:23:28.986
That requires an agency head to take on institutional risk.

00:23:28.986 --> 00:23:31.066
But they have the legal power to do it, right?

00:23:31.066 --> 00:23:33.266
The letters from both the Democrats and the Republicans

00:23:33.266 --> 00:23:34.506
are very specific about this.

00:23:34.506 --> 00:23:37.346
They aren't asking for Congress to rewrite the law.

00:23:37.346 --> 00:23:37.746
Yes.

00:23:37.746 --> 00:23:39.786
And this is where the legal strategy pushed

00:23:39.786 --> 00:23:41.986
by the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs Administration

00:23:41.986 --> 00:23:43.266
is so sharp.

00:23:43.266 --> 00:23:46.666
The letters explicitly point to section 1876,

00:23:46.666 --> 00:23:48.586
a four of the Social Security Act.

00:23:48.586 --> 00:23:51.346
Section 1876, a four, what does that actually say?

00:23:51.386 --> 00:23:53.946
Broadly, it gives the Secretary of HHS

00:23:53.946 --> 00:23:57.626
the authority to adjust Medicare Advantage payments

00:23:57.626 --> 00:24:00.186
if the raw data doesn't accurately reflect

00:24:00.186 --> 00:24:01.986
the statutory intent of the program.

00:24:01.986 --> 00:24:02.746
Oh, wow.

00:24:02.746 --> 00:24:05.546
They are telling HHS and CMS, you already

00:24:05.546 --> 00:24:07.426
have the statutory authority to fix this.

00:24:07.426 --> 00:24:11.586
You have the legal right to look at that raw, flawed, fee

00:24:11.586 --> 00:24:14.266
for service data of 29,000 people

00:24:14.266 --> 00:24:17.226
and say, this is not statistically reliable.

00:24:17.226 --> 00:24:18.946
Therefore, we are using our authority

00:24:18.946 --> 00:24:23.226
to establish a national minimum floor of 0.70

00:24:23.226 --> 00:24:25.146
to ensure equitable care.

00:24:25.146 --> 00:24:27.226
So they don't need permission.

00:24:27.226 --> 00:24:30.266
They just have to have the political and bureaucratic will

00:24:30.266 --> 00:24:31.946
to actually execute the override.

00:24:31.946 --> 00:24:32.986
Precisely.

00:24:32.986 --> 00:24:34.626
And that's why this pressure campaign

00:24:34.626 --> 00:24:36.346
had to be so meticulously built.

00:24:36.346 --> 00:24:37.546
They are trying to generate enough

00:24:37.546 --> 00:24:40.786
bipartisan, logical, math-based pressure

00:24:40.786 --> 00:24:43.146
to overcome that innate bureaucratic inertia.

00:24:43.146 --> 00:24:44.986
They have to make it more painful and publicly

00:24:44.986 --> 00:24:47.546
embarrassing for the agency to ignore the problem

00:24:47.546 --> 00:24:48.706
than it is to fix it.

00:24:48.706 --> 00:24:49.206
Exactly.

00:24:49.206 --> 00:24:50.146
Let's zoom out for a second.

00:24:50.146 --> 00:24:51.346
Let's bring this back to the listener.

00:24:51.346 --> 00:24:54.546
Because I can imagine someone listening to this deep dive

00:24:54.546 --> 00:24:57.946
right now, in Ohio or Oregon or Nebraska, thinking,

00:24:57.946 --> 00:25:00.346
well, this is fascinating policy drama.

00:25:00.346 --> 00:25:01.986
The math is crazy.

00:25:01.986 --> 00:25:04.066
But why should I care?

00:25:04.066 --> 00:25:08.466
It's a Medicare formula for an island 1,000 miles away.

00:25:08.466 --> 00:25:11.306
What does this have to do with the American taxpayer

00:25:11.306 --> 00:25:12.226
on the mainland?

00:25:12.226 --> 00:25:14.146
It has everything to do with the American taxpayer

00:25:14.146 --> 00:25:16.826
on the mainland purely from an economic and systemic

00:25:16.826 --> 00:25:17.426
standpoint.

00:25:17.426 --> 00:25:19.186
Because of the medical migration.

00:25:19.186 --> 00:25:21.146
The sources mention this explicitly,

00:25:21.146 --> 00:25:23.546
and it's something I think most people completely overlook.

00:25:23.546 --> 00:25:24.186
Yes.

00:25:24.186 --> 00:25:26.186
The GOP letter led by Dr. Murphy

00:25:26.186 --> 00:25:28.186
laid this out very clearly.

00:25:28.186 --> 00:25:30.746
When Puerto Rican providers are chronically underpaid

00:25:30.746 --> 00:25:33.746
by 41% below the national average,

00:25:33.746 --> 00:25:36.586
the health care system strains to the breaking point.

00:25:36.586 --> 00:25:39.226
Networks weaken, wait times explode,

00:25:39.226 --> 00:25:41.906
provider shortages become acute because the doctors

00:25:41.906 --> 00:25:44.386
move to Texas or Florida to make a normal living.

00:25:44.386 --> 00:25:44.986
Naturally.

00:25:44.986 --> 00:25:47.386
And what happens when an American citizen living

00:25:47.426 --> 00:25:50.426
in Puerto Rico cannot get the specialty care they need,

00:25:50.426 --> 00:25:53.386
say cancer treatment or complex cardiology?

00:25:53.386 --> 00:25:54.346
Get on a plane.

00:25:54.346 --> 00:25:55.026
Exactly.

00:25:55.026 --> 00:25:57.066
They migrate to the US mainland.

00:25:57.066 --> 00:26:00.666
Both the beneficiary seeking care and the doctors and nurses

00:26:00.666 --> 00:26:03.386
seeking fair compensation leave the island.

00:26:03.386 --> 00:26:04.746
And here is the kicker.

00:26:04.746 --> 00:26:07.226
When a Medicare beneficiary moves from Puerto Rico

00:26:07.226 --> 00:26:09.826
to Florida or New York or Ohio,

00:26:09.826 --> 00:26:11.986
they are still in the Medicare system.

00:26:11.986 --> 00:26:14.746
But now their care is being paid for at the mainland rates.

00:26:14.746 --> 00:26:15.386
Exactly.

00:26:15.386 --> 00:26:18.626
The federal government hasn't saved a single dime.

00:26:18.626 --> 00:26:18.946
Wow.

00:26:18.946 --> 00:26:21.146
In fact, the sources extrapolate on this point.

00:26:21.146 --> 00:26:23.346
This migration actually increases

00:26:23.346 --> 00:26:26.546
overall Medicare expenditures for American taxpayers.

00:26:26.546 --> 00:26:27.986
Care on the mainland is generally

00:26:27.986 --> 00:26:29.426
more expensive to begin with.

00:26:29.426 --> 00:26:29.986
Right.

00:26:29.986 --> 00:26:32.826
If the federal government had just adequately funded

00:26:32.826 --> 00:26:36.706
the system in Puerto Rico with the 0.70 benchmark,

00:26:36.706 --> 00:26:38.346
they could have treated that patient closer

00:26:38.346 --> 00:26:41.706
to home in their own community for less money overall.

00:26:41.706 --> 00:26:44.506
It's the ultimate lesson in like an ounce of prevention

00:26:44.506 --> 00:26:46.386
is worth a pound of cure.

00:26:46.386 --> 00:26:48.906
Underfunding Puerto Rico doesn't save the federal government

00:26:48.906 --> 00:26:51.546
money, it just shifts a much higher cost

00:26:51.546 --> 00:26:53.546
to the mainland health care system.

00:26:53.546 --> 00:26:55.826
It is a massive false economy.

00:26:55.826 --> 00:26:58.626
A false economy that simultaneously destabilizes

00:26:58.626 --> 00:27:01.746
the territory of over 3 million American citizens.

00:27:01.746 --> 00:27:03.546
A strong Puerto Rican health care system

00:27:03.546 --> 00:27:06.666
is fundamentally a more cost-effective and resilient

00:27:06.666 --> 00:27:08.906
system for the entire federal government.

00:27:08.906 --> 00:27:11.026
So let's summarize where we are.

00:27:11.066 --> 00:27:15.186
We started with 676,000 US citizens relying

00:27:15.186 --> 00:27:17.786
on Medicare Advantage, effectively trapped

00:27:17.786 --> 00:27:22.026
by an outdated federal math problem, a broken thermometer.

00:27:22.026 --> 00:27:24.626
A formula that uses 3% of the population

00:27:24.626 --> 00:27:27.626
to dictate the survival of the other 97%,

00:27:27.626 --> 00:27:30.266
ignoring the fact that those 3% are primarily veterans

00:27:30.266 --> 00:27:31.386
and low utilizers.

00:27:31.386 --> 00:27:34.626
But we also saw a masterclass in how to fight back.

00:27:34.626 --> 00:27:37.226
We saw the coordinated, relentless push

00:27:37.226 --> 00:27:39.826
by the Puerto Rico Federal Affairs Administration

00:27:39.826 --> 00:27:42.466
aligning with the private sector realities of the Health Care

00:27:42.466 --> 00:27:43.546
Coalition.

00:27:43.546 --> 00:27:45.466
They took a deeply technical grievance,

00:27:45.466 --> 00:27:47.146
paired it with the terrifying reality

00:27:47.146 --> 00:27:50.386
of an impending Medicaid cliff, and translated it

00:27:50.386 --> 00:27:54.186
into a viable, bipartisan, administrative fix,

00:27:54.186 --> 00:27:57.186
the 0.70 benchmark floor.

00:27:57.186 --> 00:27:59.946
They bypassed the legislative gridlock.

00:27:59.946 --> 00:28:02.186
They brought in doctors like Rep Greg Murphy

00:28:02.186 --> 00:28:03.786
and pharmacists like Rep Luddy Carter

00:28:03.786 --> 00:28:06.026
to validate the clinical reality.

00:28:06.026 --> 00:28:07.466
And they pointed the administration

00:28:07.466 --> 00:28:09.506
directly to its own statutory authority

00:28:09.506 --> 00:28:11.986
to fix a glaring anomaly.

00:28:11.986 --> 00:28:14.346
It is a testament to what institutional coordination

00:28:14.346 --> 00:28:17.146
can achieve when the math is undeniably on your side.

00:28:17.146 --> 00:28:18.346
It really is.

00:28:18.346 --> 00:28:20.106
And before we close out this deep dive,

00:28:20.106 --> 00:28:22.986
I want to leave our listeners with something to mull over,

00:28:22.986 --> 00:28:25.506
because this story, as complex as it is,

00:28:25.506 --> 00:28:29.986
points to a much bigger, slightly unsettling reality

00:28:29.986 --> 00:28:32.026
about how our world works today.

00:28:32.026 --> 00:28:32.546
It does.

00:28:32.546 --> 00:28:36.066
This entire crisis stems from a well-intentioned federal

00:28:36.066 --> 00:28:38.986
formula that simply didn't account for the unique reality

00:28:38.986 --> 00:28:41.986
on the ground, and a bureaucracy terrified

00:28:41.986 --> 00:28:44.946
to manually override its own flawed code.

00:28:44.946 --> 00:28:47.586
It makes you wonder, what other outdated algorithms

00:28:47.586 --> 00:28:49.626
or statistical formulas are quietly

00:28:49.626 --> 00:28:51.626
running in the background of our government,

00:28:51.626 --> 00:28:54.106
dictating funding for education, infrastructure,

00:28:54.106 --> 00:28:56.546
or agriculture, completely detached

00:28:56.546 --> 00:28:59.106
from the reality of the people they're supposed to serve?

00:28:59.106 --> 00:28:59.666
Right.

00:28:59.666 --> 00:29:01.306
We assume the system is giving us

00:29:01.306 --> 00:29:04.146
a precise measurement, a clear picture.

00:29:04.146 --> 00:29:06.626
But sometimes you really have to stop and ask,

00:29:06.626 --> 00:29:08.146
who calibrated the machine, and what

00:29:08.186 --> 00:29:09.586
are they actually measuring?

00:29:09.586 --> 00:29:11.426
Food for thought.

